tags: []
date: 2026-03-23T00:00:00-04:00
---

The sewers beneath [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/index|Hallia]] are made to carry the city's waste and are not built to be defended; there are many ways in and out. A manhole in the open-air market of [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Widdershins/index|Widdershins]] drops onto a pumping floor that feeds into the sewer proper, and the [[Anti-Zorbon Taskforce|Anti-Zorbon Taskforce]] kept a hideout further along.

Past the hideout a slow underground river runs. It is an offshoot of the Fousan that either rejoins the river inside the city or runs on down into the earth, since no second river leaves Hallia. A makeshift dock beside the water was built to move beneath the city unseen, and the Party rode it downriver in a boat.

The river leaves the sewers and runs into wild, unlit caves. It widens into a circular cavern about a hundred feet across, where a narrow three-foot ledge rings a central pit and the water plunges out of sight. The cave walls are craggy. On the far side of the pit a lift is built into the rock, and a two-hundred-foot ladder climbs from its platform to a heavy iron hatch that opens back into another stretch of the sewers. The platform is a staging area still in use, stocked with hard hats that carry lamps, flashlights, nutrition bars, food and water, and marked by no organization.

Below this the lift descends for about fifteen minutes in the dark to a cavern where purple crystals on the ceiling cast a bluish glow. Two giant serpents live in the pools there. They speak, and before they would let the Party pass they wanted to know who they were and to see a permission slip.

Closer to [[Geography/Calaria/Hallia/Teller's Run/index|Teller's Run]] the sewers change. The newer tunnels are [[Org/Prime Meridian/index|Prime Meridian]]'s, laid in the last century; nearer the district's border the Party passed into the older works built by the Calarian royal family before it was deposed. Those tunnels have raised platforms on either side, so a body can walk clear of the flow, and no street grates, so they carry their own light. The walls are cobbled and catacomb-like and the refuse is thick in the air.

Where the tunnel opens out near the border there is a maintained sewage works, occupied and kept up but with nobody in sight when the Party came through. It looks like a parking lot: a staging area with stacked crates and one or two small boats tied along the water, enough flow that a shallow craft could pass. A shrine has been built around a machine that glows blue, and the machine does not touch the sewer around it. The puddles by the shrine move when nobody is looking. When [[People/Anzu|Anzu]] pulled the heat from one, it spasmed, and the others rose out of the water into oozes, jellies, tar and slimes.

The Party fought them where the tunnel opened out. The puddles had been oozes in disguise: [[Bestiary/Ochre Jelly|ochre jellies]] that came apart in two when cut and melted to water when they died, a [[Bestiary/Gray Ooze|gray ooze]], and one thing of [[Bestiary/Living Tar|living tar]] that held the blades that struck it and drew rocks into itself to throw. When it was over, water on the ground was most of what was left of them.

The shrine stands at the center of the clearing. A seated figure of stone, about eight feet from where it sits to the top of its head, robed and androgynous, with two points where horns would be: the right straight and pointing up, the left cut off and sanded down to a flat stump. Tiny humanoids, each about an eighth of an inch tall and carved from the same stone, are spread across the floor around it and climbing its legs. Argyle found magic gathered in the figure the way it gathers in a place where people have prayed, and nothing else in the shrine is magic. The figure matches no god or inner god he knows, and is not fiendish, demonic or devilish; he called it the work of some unrelated cult. Its magic vibrates in the same style as the tree at the [[Org/Gryphonline/index|Gryphonline]] post office and the [[Phenomena/Radar Dishes|radar dishes]], though it oscillates differently. Anzu sketched it.

The blue light in the machinery is not the machines giving off light. Behind glass, a glowing blue liquid runs through channels in them like veins, fast enough to see. In the pillars where it rises it is a darker blue that gives off little light; it reaches the top, something happens, and it comes back down glowing much brighter. Much of the machinery is pumps, gathering magic from somewhere above and carrying it below. The metal is an alloy of iron and lead, which holds magic in; without it the place would be a beacon. The blue is the same the Party had seen in guns from [[Org/Sable and Suns/index|Sable and Suns]] and in the light one of the oozes threw.

Past the shrine the tunnel runs on into a straight hall sixty feet across, lined with the same pillars, with no end in sight. The sound of running water hangs at the edge of hearing and seems to grow louder and recede at once. The farther the Party walked the lighter they felt, and the rate at which they grew lighter picked up the longer they walked. After about ten minutes, roughly a mile, they judged they were about three quarters of the way through Teller's Run. They turned back, and their weight returned as they went.

Ten feet ahead of where they stopped is a hairline crack in the air, like a fracture in a clear pane of glass, with the hall apparently running on beyond it. Anzu's magic told him it gives off more magic than anything he had felt, too much to be measured. The Party took it for a smaller version of the crack in the [[Phenomena/Firmament|firmament]]. When Anzu reached a hand toward it, he got within about six inches before the world around him seemed to crack and his sight splintered; he pulled back and it healed. The crack does not open, and there is no space to pass through.
